---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import CTABand from '../components/CTABand.astro';
import Starburst from '../components/Starburst.astro';
import AtomMark from '../components/AtomMark.astro';
---
<Base
  title="About 33Mega | Every human. 33 megatons of good."
  description="33Mega was founded by Peter Watling and Jonathan Morgan so organisations never have to choose between the freedom of open technology and the confidence of a fully supported product."
>
  <!-- Origin story -->
  <section class="band halftone">
    <div class="section-inner grid-2" style="align-items: center;">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">The name</p>
        <h1>Every human. 33 megatons of good.</h1>
        <p class="lead">
          When Jonathan was a child, someone told him that an atom bomb carried a megaton of
          destruction — but that people, together, were capable of thirty-three times that much
          good. The idea stuck.
        </p>
        <p>
          33Mega is named for it: a reminder of what technology is for. Our job is to build
          infrastructure that quietly does its work, so the people who rely on it — storytellers,
          archivists, broadcasters, engineers — are free to do theirs.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="origin-art" aria-hidden="true">
        <Starburst class="origin-burst" color="#e8a33d" />
        <AtomMark size={260} class="origin-atom" />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Mission -->
  <section class="band white">
    <div class="section-inner center">
      <p class="eyebrow">The mission</p>
      <h2 style="max-width: 860px; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto;">
        Organisations should never have to choose between the freedom of open technology and the
        confidence of a fully supported product.
      </h2>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Why we started -->
  <section class="band">
    <div class="section-inner grid-2">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Why we started 33Mega</p>
        <h2>Two compromises we refused to keep making.</h2>
        <p>
          For years, organisations buying storage have faced a choice between two compromises.
        </p>
        <p>
          On one side sit the traditional enterprise vendors. Their platforms work, but at a
          price: proprietary hardware, proprietary software licences, and upgrade cycles dictated by
          someone else's roadmap. Migrating away gets harder every year as the data estate grows.
        </p>
        <p>
          On the other side sits open-source software. Platforms like Ceph have matured into some
          of the most capable storage technologies in existence — they run at CERN, inside
          hyperscale clouds, and across more than 10,000 deployments worldwide. But open source
          is a platform, not a product. Running it well takes senior storage engineers, and most
          media organisations don't have that team and can't justify hiring one.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Proprietary platforms are expensive and restrictive; running open source
          yourself is hard. 33Mega is the bridge between those two worlds.</strong>
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="pop-panel gold">
        <h3>What we believe</h3>
        <p class="small" style="color: var(--ink);">We keep our beliefs short, because we intend to be held to them.</p>
        <ul class="check-list">
          <li><strong>Customers should own their data.</strong> Open formats, standard protocols, hardware you own — including the freedom to leave us.</li>
          <li><strong>Support matters more than licensing.</strong> We charge for the engineering and support that keep storage healthy — not for locking you into a proprietary product.</li>
          <li><strong>Infrastructure should last.</strong> We extend the life of hardware rather than engineer reasons to replace it.</li>
          <li><strong>Lean is a feature.</strong> We run a small company deliberately, so customers pay for engineering and support, not overhead.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Founders -->
  <section class="band white" id="founders">
    <div class="section-inner">
      <div class="center">
        <p class="eyebrow">Built on experience</p>
        <h2>The founders.</h2>
        <p class="lead" style="max-width: 800px; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto;">
          Peter Watling and Jonathan Morgan met at Object Matrix, the media storage company
          Jonathan founded and led. Between them: decades of designing, delivering and supporting
          storage and media workflows for broadcasters, post-production facilities, sports
          organisations, archives and content creators.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="grid-2" style="margin-top: 44px;">
        <div class="founder pop-panel">
          <img class="founder-photo" src="/images/peter-watling.jpg" alt="Peter Watling, co-founder of 33Mega, in pop-art style on his bike" width="360" height="450" loading="lazy" />
          <h3>Peter Watling</h3>
          <p class="small">Co-founder · Commercial</p>
          <p>
            Peter is an advocate for innovative technology companies and has spent his career
            getting media technology into the hands of the people who need it. Recently he founded
            a media business consultancy to help international companies expand into new regions.
          </p>
          <p>
            At Object Matrix he led EMEA sales and also established a cloud storage service that
            became a strategic part of the business. Following the acquisition by DataCore he ran
            the media operation across the region.
          </p>
          <p>
            Off the clock he is a serious cyclist, and the founder of Velokom, a charitable company
            that supports the families of riders seriously injured or lost on the road.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="founder pop-panel">
          <img class="founder-photo" src="/images/jonathan-morgan.jpg" alt="Jonathan Morgan, co-founder of 33Mega, in pop-art comic style" width="360" height="360" loading="lazy" />
          <h3>Jonathan Morgan</h3>
          <p class="small">Co-founder · Product &amp; Technology</p>
          <p>
            Jonathan is an entrepreneur and technology leader based in Cardiff. He founded and led
            Object Matrix, the company that pioneered object storage for the media industry, and
            after its acquisition held SVP Product and Technology responsibility across Object
            Matrix, Swarm and OpenEBS at Perifery/DataCore.
          </p>
          <p>
            His work spans product strategy, AI, cloud services, object storage and media
            workflows — and the practical leadership needed to get from architecture diagrams to
            systems that run for years. The "33 megatons" story is his; the standard it sets
            belongs to the whole company.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Different -->
  <section class="band">
    <div class="section-inner grid-2">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">What makes 33Mega different</p>
        <h2>Claims you can check.</h2>
        <p>
          Every storage vendor claims to hate lock-in. We think the claim only counts when you
          can test it, so here is ours in checkable form:
        </p>
        <ul class="check-list">
          <li>Our platforms are built on Ceph — open-source software proven at CERN, not code we control.</li>
          <li>The hardware is standard equipment from Dell, bought on the open market and owned by you.</li>
          <li>Data is stored in open formats and served over standard protocols, including S3.</li>
          <li>We document how to leave — off-boarding is written down, not discovered.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          What we add is everything that makes open source usable as a product: hardware
          validation, design, build, integration with your workflows, 24×7 monitoring, managed
          upgrades, and enterprise support with a four-hour software SLA — delivered by people
          who have spent their careers in media.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="pop-panel pink" id="leave">
        <h3>If you ever leave us</h3>
        <p><em>This is the section no vendor writes, so we will.</em></p>
        <p>
          If 33Mega disappeared tomorrow, or you simply chose to go elsewhere: your hardware is
          yours — standard and reusable. Your data sits in Ceph, open-source software with a
          large global community and multiple commercial support providers. Your applications
          talk to it over standard S3 and file protocols. Off-boarding is documented, not
          discovered.
        </p>
        <p style="margin-bottom: 0;">
          <strong>We believe making it easy to leave is the best reason to stay.</strong>
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Looking forward -->
  <section class="band white">
    <div class="section-inner center">
      <p class="eyebrow">Looking forward</p>
      <h2 style="max-width: 820px; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto;">
        We're not trying to be the biggest storage company. We're trying to be the most trusted.
      </h2>
      <p class="lead" style="max-width: 720px; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto;">
        The company media organisations call because the platform works, the support answers,
        and the exit door is never locked. When infrastructure quietly does its job, people are
        free to do theirs. That's the idea the company is named after.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <CTABand />
</Base>

<style>
  .origin-art {
    position: relative;
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    min-height: 320px;
  }
  .origin-art :global(.origin-burst) {
    position: absolute;
    width: 400px;
    height: 400px;
  }
  .origin-art :global(.origin-atom) {
    position: relative;
    filter: drop-shadow(5px 5px 0 rgba(23, 18, 31, 0.22));
  }
  .founder-photo {
    width: 100%;
    max-width: 360px;
    border: var(--border);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    margin-bottom: 18px;
    object-fit: cover;
  }
  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .origin-art { min-height: 230px; }
    .origin-art :global(.origin-burst) { width: 260px; height: 260px; }
    .origin-art :global(.origin-atom) { width: 180px; height: 180px; }
  }
</style>
